    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • Project KO11 Is Proof That Geely Wants To \"Knock Out\" Range Rover, Launches Next Year in Europe\nCanadians Lean European on New Cars, Many Warm Up to Electric Vehicles\n13 photos\nPhoto: Renault\nCommissioned by Clean Energy Canada, a new survey from Abacus Data suggests that 47 percent of Canadians prefer a Euro-leaning new car market, especially when it comes to electric vehicles and smaller footprints than what they're accustomed to from the United States of America.\nBe that as it may, 21 percent would rather see more American influence on Canadian roads. The margin is better than two to one, and the people who commissioned the polling read it as demand the current shelf does not fill. Abacus Data surveyed 2,762 adults online between September 18 and September 23, weighted to match census data on relevant metrics, and made it work with a 1.86-point margin error.\nBefore picking a direction, respondents were asked by Abacus Data to think through what each one would mean for the brands, models, segments, and electric options actually available to them at the time of the survey. What the remaining share of the sample wanted goes unstated in the survey, leaving the status quo with whatever is left after the aforementioned 47 and 21 percent are counted.\nThe demand behind the preference is electric, and it appears to be growing. 48 percent of Canadians now say their next automobile is likely going to be fully electric, up three percentage points from the 45 measured in April 2026 and last year. Age bends the curve a lot, with youngsters aged 18 to 29 more inclined to go electric than 30 to 44-year-olds.\nWhat holds many potential EV customers back is diversity. Among likely electric vehicle buyers, 42 percent would rather wait for more Chinese alternatives to reach Canadian dealers. 37 percent believe there are too few electric pickups on offer, and they're completely right on that one. However, the lack of demand for such vehicles is clearly visible in Q3 2026 releases from all manufacturers who offer electric trucks in Canada.\nPhoto: Renault The gap between what buyers know about the product and what the market sells also deserves some attention. Upfront price consistently ranks as the most prohibitive barrier to going electric, hence the pitiful demand for electric trucks. With a bit of luck, Ford's all-new Fathom will democratize the electric pickup for both the US market and Canada.\nThe survey also found that Canadians are poorly informed about how far prices have already come. 60 percent of potential buyers are at least somewhat aware that new EVs can be purchased in the $30,000 to $40,000 range, but only one in five is very aware of those options. Used electric vehicles fare much worse. Awareness of the $5,000 federal rebate on eligible new EVs stands at 62 percent.\n69 percent, meanwhile, know that EVs are generally cheaper to own and drive. Said figures suggest that a good chunk of the affordability question is an information problem. All in all, 2027 will be a pivotal year for Canada's electric vehicle market due to the arrival of the Fathom and the introduction of additional Chinese models, including a rumored pair of models from Geely .\nThe Abacus Data survey, as presented by Clean Energy Canada, carried a small test of whether the Canadian appetite for European rules is actually real. Canadians were told that European law requires public chargers to accept credit/debit cards without an app, which is completely different from Canada's app-happy charging infrastructure. 69 percent of respondents agreed that Canadian chargers should be required to work the same way.\nAnd finally, politics also point in the same direction. European Commission prez Ursula von der Leyen, who is particularly vilified across Europe if you are to believe the social media nonsense spewed by Russian bot farms, invited Canada in September 2026 to become the bloc's first associate member. The EU-Canada summit at the end of October 2026 could very well advance the relationship, bringing Canada closer to Europe's auto industry.\nVia // Clean Energy Canada\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nCanada Europe EV survey poll\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Man Trying To Sell His Two 1968 Chargers for 10 Months, the Offer Has Since Changed",
